#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Overview {#overview}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Q-CTRL **Dynamics Simulator** is an application Qiskit Function for "
"simulating the time evolution of supported quantum many-body models on IBM "
"Quantum hardware. Instead of constructing quantum circuits yourself, you "
"specify the physical model, the initial state, the time-evolution "
"parameters, and the observables to measure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The function constructs and executes the required dynamic circuits and "
"applies Fire Opal's hardware-aware optimization and error suppression "
"workflow. The result is a time trace of expectation values for the "
"observables you requested. Because the function applies error suppression "
"automatically, you can run circuits at record depths and still see "
"meaningful signals. The Dynamics Simulator delivers solution accuracy that "
"matches the existing state-of-the-art classical tools and gives a "
"demonstrable advantage in speed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Real-world testing achieved agreement within 1% root mean square error "
"(RMSE) when compared against the highest-resolution classical tensor-network"
" simulations. [Independent tests](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.04771) from the"
" research community have validated that the quantum computer remains "
"accurate even beyond Q-CTRL's initial demonstrations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Two plots of classical tensor-network simulations at bond dimensions χ "
"from 64 to 4096. The left plot shows the RMSE against time t, which stays "
"near 0.01 until it rises sharply, later for larger bond dimensions. The "
"right plot shows the simulation time in minutes against time t on a "
"logarithmic scale, which increases with bond "
"dimension.](/docs/images/guides/q-ctrl-dynamics-simulator/tensor-network-"
"comparison.svg \"Agreement between quantum and classical simulations as the "
"“resolution” of the classical simulation increases. As the resolution "
"increases, both the agreement with the quantum computer and the classical-"
"simulation runtime increase. Quantum computer runtimes are faster by up to "
"3000 times.\")\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Benchmarks {#benchmarks}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The number of sites determines how many qubits are needed, while the number "
"of Trotter steps controls the depth of the time-evolution circuits. Each "
"lattice site uses two qubits to represent the spin-up and spin-down "
"occupations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This workflow has been demonstrated for chains of 60 sites (120 qubits) with"
" 30 Trotter steps, and for chains of 31 sites (62 qubits) with 90 Trotter "
"steps. The table below shows the circuit resources and QPU execution times "
"from the [reported experiments](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.04025), performed"
" on IBM&reg; Heron r3 (`ibm_boston`) device with 20,000 shots per circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| System size | Trotter steps | Two-qubit depth | Two-qubit gate count | QPU time | QPU time with REM + DR |\n"
"| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n"
"| 10 sites (20 qubits) | 30 | 152 | 1,457 | 2m 46s | 4m 25s |\n"
"| 10 sites (20 qubits) | 60 | 302 | 2,897 | 5m 36s | 8m 42s |\n"
"| 10 sites (20 qubits) | 90 | 452 | 4,337 | 8m 46s | 13m 26s |\n"
"| 31 sites (62 qubits) | 30 | 152 | 4,649 | 2m 46s | 4m 25s |\n"
"| 31 sites (62 qubits) | 60 | 302 | 9,239 | 5m 36s | 8m 42s |\n"
"| 31 sites (62 qubits) | 90 | 452 | 13,829 | 8m 46s | 13m 26s |\n"
"| 60 sites (120 qubits) | 30 | 152 | 9,057 | 2m 46s | 4m 25s |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For a fixed number of Trotter steps, the reported circuit depth and QPU time"
" remain the same across the tested system sizes, while the two-qubit gate "
"count increases. Increasing the number of steps produces deeper circuits and"
" requires more QPU time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here, QPU time is the cumulative hardware execution time for the main simulation circuits. The last column also includes the QPU time for noise characterization used in readout error mitigation (REM) and decay recovery (DR).\n"
"This guide uses a small one-dimensional Fermi-Hubbard example to show the complete workflow from defining the model to plotting the measured dynamics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Function description {#function-description}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Quantum dynamics describes how a quantum system changes over time. Studying "
"this evolution helps you understand how particles move, how correlations "
"develop, and how a system responds to a change in its state or "
"Hamiltonian.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Fermi-Hubbard model describes fermions moving between lattice sites and "
"interacting when they occupy the same site. The competition between hopping "
"and interactions makes it a useful model for studying strongly correlated "
"electrons. Its time evolution can reveal how spin and charge propagate, and "
"how an initially ordered system relaxes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Model {#model}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Dynamics Simulator supports a one-dimensional Fermi-Hubbard chain with "
"open boundaries. For a chain of $L$ sites, the Hamiltonian is defined as "
"follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"H =\n"
"-\\sum_{i=0}^{L-2}\\sum_{\\sigma\\in\\{\\uparrow,\\downarrow\\}}\n"
"t_{h,i}\n"
"\\left(\n"
"c^\\dagger_{i\\sigma}c_{i+1,\\sigma}\n"
"+\n"
"c^\\dagger_{i+1,\\sigma}c_{i\\sigma}\n"
"\\right)\n"
"+\\sum_{i=0}^{L-1}U_i n_{i\\uparrow}n_{i\\downarrow}\n"
"-\\sum_{i=0}^{L-1}\\sum_{\\sigma\\in\\{\\uparrow,\\downarrow\\}}\n"
"\\mu_i n_{i\\sigma}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here, $c^\\dagger_{i\\sigma}$ and $c_{i\\sigma}$ create and annihilate a "
"fermion with spin $\\sigma$ at site $i$. The number operator "
"$n_{i\\sigma}=c^\\dagger_{i\\sigma}c_{i\\sigma}$ measures the occupation of "
"that spin orbital.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The model inputs specify the following coefficients:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`hopping_amplitude`: $t_{h,i}$, which controls hopping between neighboring "
"sites $i$ and $i+1$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`on_site_interaction`: $U_i$, the interaction between spin-up and spin-down "
"fermions occupying the same site.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`chemical_potential`: $\\mu_i$, the local chemical potential, which enters "
"the Hamiltonian with a minus sign.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Supply a scalar to use the same coefficient throughout the chain, or a list "
"to specify coefficients for individual bonds or sites.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In units where $\\hbar=1$, the initial state evolves as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi(t)\\rangle=e^{-iHt}|\\psi(0)\\rangle.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The function estimates the expectation values of the observables you specify"
" at successive simulation times.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Workflow {#workflow}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The function's workflow consists of the following steps:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Algorithmic synthesis:** The function uses the Hamiltonian and simulation "
"time settings to construct an optimized Trotter-Suzuki sequence, which "
"reduces circuit depth while accounting for simulation accuracy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Hardware-aware mapping:** The function maps and routes the circuits by "
"using the qubit connectivity and error characteristics of the selected IBM "
"Quantum backend.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Physical-layer error suppression:** Fire Opal incorporates dynamical "
"decoupling and pulse-level corrections to suppress noise from the "
"environment and crosstalk during hardware execution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The function manages these stages automatically and returns the expectation "
"values of the requested observables over time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You define the physical problem and simulation settings through the function"
" inputs. The current interface supports Trotter time evolution, where "
"`time_step` sets the duration of each step and `step_count` sets the number "
"of steps.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For additional details, see the [Fire Opal dynamics simulator "
"overview](https://docs.q-ctrl.com/fire-opal/execute/run-algorithms/fire-"
"opal-dynamics-simulator) and the [`simulate_dynamics` API "
"reference](https://docs.q-ctrl.com/references/fire-"
"opal/fireopal/fireopal.simulate_dynamics).\n"
msgstr ""

