"site-fermi-hubbard-chain}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Consider a four-site one-dimensional Fermi-Hubbard chain with hopping "
"amplitude $t_h=1$, no on-site interaction, and zero chemical potential. A "
"single spin-up fermion begins at site 0 and evolves across the chain.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This small problem keeps the focus on the Dynamics Simulator workflow:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Define the model and simulation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Define the initial state and observables.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Submit the Qiskit Function job.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Retrieve the expectation values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '5. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Plot the dynamics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Define the problem {#1-define-the-problem}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The observable list measures the spin-up occupation\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle n_{i\\uparrow} \\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"at each site. The observables are returned in the same order in which they "
"are supplied.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Run the dynamics simulation {#2-run-the-dynamics-simulation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Submit the problem through the Qiskit Function. The Qiskit Functions Catalog"
" handles IBM Quantum authentication, so you don't pass separate Fire Opal or"
" hardware-provider credentials to `sim_qf.run()`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The returned object is a Qiskit Function job. You can keep the job ID to "
"retrieve or inspect the workload later.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. Retrieve the result {#3-retrieve-the-result}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Convert the observable expectation values to a NumPy array. Each row "
"corresponds to one observable, and each column corresponds to a recorded "
"simulation time, beginning at $t=0$. For this example, the four rows contain"
" the spin-up occupations of sites 0 through 3.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4. Visualize the dynamics {#4-visualize-the-dynamics}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The initial state contains one spin-up fermion and no spin-down fermions. "
"Its evolution therefore remains in the single-particle subspace, whose four "
"basis states correspond to the fermion occupying sites 0, 1, 2, and 3.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this subspace, the Hamiltonian connects neighboring sites with hopping "
"amplitude $-t_h$. For the parameters used here,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"H_{\\mathrm{1p}}\n"
"=\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"-t_h\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n"
"1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\\n"
"0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\\n"
"0 & 0 & 1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Diagonalize this matrix with NumPy and evolve the initial state at the same "
"times that were returned by the Dynamics Simulator. The squared magnitude of"
" each state amplitude gives the spin-up occupation of the corresponding "
"site.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This calculation gives exact Hamiltonian evolution. Differences between the "
"hardware estimates and this reference can include the finite Trotter step, "
"measurement statistics, and residual hardware errors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Plot one panel for each site. The solid curves show the exact reference at "
"the recorded times, and the markers show the hardware estimates returned by "
"the Dynamics Simulator.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/guides/q-ctrl-dynamics-"
"simulator/extracted-outputs/61a7ad6e-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"At $t=0$, the exact spin-up occupation is 1 at site 0 and 0 at the other "
"sites. As the system evolves, hopping spreads the particle's probability "
"across the chain. The occupation of site 1 increases first, followed by "
"increasing occupation at sites 2 and 3. For the parameters in this example, "
"the final recorded time is $t=1.6$. The exact occupations of sites 0 through"
" 3 are approximately 0.026, 0.370, 0.392, and 0.211, respectively.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The exact occupations satisfy the following equation at every time, "
"reflecting conservation of particle number:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\sum_{i=0}^{3}\\langle n_{i\\uparrow}(t)\\rangle=1\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Individual site occupations can rise and fall as the particle evolves "
"through the finite chain.\n"
msgstr ""

